From: OTB V5 Build <root@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: mediatek: keep exact-DIP HW LRO below uppers

Linux deliberately clears NETIF_F_LRO on a lower device when a bond or a
bridge above it does not advertise LRO.  This is the correct behaviour for
generic LRO because an arbitrary aggregated packet must not be forwarded.

The MediaTek HW-LRO implementation is different when an RX classifier rule
has been explicitly installed: hardware only aggregates TCP/IPv4 packets
whose destination address exactly matches a programmed DIP.  Keep these
explicit rules in the global MT7988 DIP table even when NETIF_F_LRO has been
cleared by a bond or bridge.  Unmatched and forwarded traffic remains on the
normal RSS rings, and no rule means that HW-LRO remains inactive.

This makes filtered HW-LRO usable for traffic terminated locally on a
bridged 802.3ad interface without enabling unsafe generic LRO on the bridge
or its bond.

Signed-off-by: OTB V5 Build <root@localhost>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
@@ -3425,15 +3425,24 @@ mtk_hwlro_sync_ipaddr(struct mtk_eth *eth, struct net_device *override_dev,
 
 		if (!dev)
 			continue;
 
+		mac = netdev_priv(dev);
 		enabled = !!(dev->features & NETIF_F_LRO);
 		if (dev == override_dev)
 			enabled = override_enabled;
-		if (!enabled)
+
+		/*
+		 * A bond or bridge upper clears NETIF_F_LRO recursively.
+		 * Explicit HW classifier rules are exact TCP/IPv4 destination
+		 * matches, so they remain safe for locally terminated traffic:
+		 * unmatched traffic, including forwarded traffic, stays on the
+		 * normal RSS rings.  A rule is therefore also an explicit
+		 * opt-in to filtered HW-LRO below an upper device.
+		 */
+		if (!enabled && !mac->hwlro_ip_cnt)
 			continue;
 
-		mac = netdev_priv(dev);
 		for (j = 0; j < MTK_MAX_LRO_IP_CNT; j++) {
 			u32 ip = mac->hwlro_ip[j];
 
 			if (!ip)
